}

{
  "loaded": [
    {
      "tool_id": "hirearchy::propose_org",
      "description": "Validate a blueprint and return the plan the user must approve. Writes nothing; safe to call repeatedly.\n\n        blueprint: YAML or JSON text (never a file path). Start from blueprint_example(kind) for a template with every\n        field explained. Schema: `mission` (one paragraph); `needs` {scope, deliverable {description, format,\n        max_words, path (absolute)}, quality_bar, sources, inputs (read-only folders), workspace (the one absolute\n        folder agents may write in), budget_runs (the user's cap), models {cheap, strong} (optional: none = every agent on claude-sonnet-5.5), model_policy, review\n        (true/false), approval, progress, kind (research|writing|code), code {tests, packaging, commits}}; `agents`:\n        ONE top-level agent (template manager) with `children`; each agent has name and a template (manager,\n        researcher, writer, reviewer, fact-checker, engineer, tester, code-reviewer, analyst, data-checker) or a charter, and optionally\n        model, tools, workspace, scale; `task` {to, title, goal, acceptance: list of exact, checkable criteria}; optional\n        `services` (recurring work the top agent offers on request): [{name, description, inputs: [{name, description,\n        required}], acceptance, deliverable {description, format, max_words, path}, budget (runs per request), playbook,\n        locked}], in which case `task` may be omitted or be a first request {service, inputs: {name: value}, title}.\n        Works before the organization exists. Returns (1) a block headed \"PLAN FOR YOU\": show it to the user exactly\n        as written and wait for their explicit approval; (2) Details, the full plan for you; (3) a plan_id that\n        apply_org requires. If the plan has errors, fix the blueprint and propose again; never ask the user to approve\n        a plan with errors.\n        ",
      "input_schema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "blueprint": {
            "title": "Blueprint",
            "type": "string"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "blueprint"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "tool_id": "hirearchy::create_org",
      "description": "Create the organization this server is bound to (only possible while none exists).\n\n        mission: what the organization is for, in a few sentences. default_model: kiro model for all agents\n        (optional; unset = claude-sonnet-5.5). path: absolute directory; only needed (and only accepted) when the server is not bound to\n        one by ORG_ROOT. The directory must be missing or empty. Afterwards every tool works, no restart needed.\n        ",
      "input_schema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "mission": {
            "title": "Mission",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "default_model": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "type": "string"
              },
              {
                "type": "null"
              }
            ],
            "default": null,
            "title": "Default Model"
          },
          "path": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "type": "string"
              },
              {
                "type": "null"
              }
            ],
            "default": null,
            "title": "Path"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "mission"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "tool_id": "hirearchy::apply_org",
      "description": "Create the agents of a blueprint and give its first task. Only after the user approved the plan.\n\n        blueprint: the YAML/JSON text the user approved via propose_org (unchanged). approved: pass True only after\n        the user explicitly approved that plan in this conversation; otherwise nothing happens. plan_id: the id that\n        propose_org returned for exactly this blueprint; a missing or different id is refused (propose again, show the\n        PLAN FOR YOU block, get approval). Needs an existing organization (create_org first if there is none).\n        Refuses with the issues if the blueprint has errors or a name is taken, and undoes everything if it fails\n        midway. Returns what was created (agents, services, the first task or request); then call start() (not when\n        the blueprint gave no first task: the services wait for request(service, inputs)).\n        ",
      "input_schema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "blueprint": {
            "title": "Blueprint",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "approved": {
            "default": false,
            "title": "Approved",
            "type": "boolean"
          },
          "plan_id": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "type": "string"
              },
              {
                "type": "null"
              }
            ],
            "default": null,
            "title": "Plan Id"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "blueprint"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "tool_id": "hirearchy::start",
      "description": "Start the scheduler for this organization in the background; it wakes agents that have mail.\n\n        max_runs: stop starting agent runs after this many (safety cap; default: no cap). until_done (default True):\n        the scheduler exits by itself when you have no open task and nothing is running or can run, so you need not\n        stop it (status and progress then say \"scheduler: stopped (finished: no open tasks)\"); start it again after\n        giving the org more work. It also exits by itself, after a few minutes (`until_done_grace_seconds`), when every\n        open task is waiting for YOU (an agent is out of budget or keeps failing, or a task is stalled): status and\n        progress then say \"stopped (waiting on you: ...)\" with your options. start() refuses while that is the case:\n        grant runs first (send(to=<agent>, body=<why>, budget=N)). False keeps it running until stop(). Refused if one\n        is already running. Output goes to .runtime/scheduler.log. Returns the scheduler's pid.\n        ",
      "input_schema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "max_runs": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "type": "integer"
              },
              {
                "type": "null"
              }
            ],
            "default": null,
            "title": "Max Runs"
          },
          "until_done": {
            "default": true,
            "title": "Until Done",
            "type": "boolean"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "tool_id": "hirearchy::progress",
      "description": "One plain status line per task you (root) gave, then what needs your decision, then the scheduler. Read-only.\n\n        Per task: working / waiting on <agent> / stalled / blocked: <reason> / done / failed, who is busy, runs used\n        of the budget given (all runs of the task's team since it was given, against its budget plus every later grant),\n        time since the last activity, and for finished tasks the artifact paths and the runs returned. \"Needs you\"\n        lists every unread note, escalation, budget request, blocked/failed result and runtime notice, with its id.\n        task_id: only that task, in any state (default: open tasks and those finished in the last day).\n        wait_seconds (0-900, default 0): first wait until a task finishes, fails, blocks or stalls, or something new\n        needs the user, or the time is up, then answer; the answer starts with what changed. A task counts as in\n        progress, so the wait really waits, while its state is working/waiting, and also while it is blocked or\n        stalled but an agent below it is running or can run (unread mail that wakes it, budget left): for example the\n        lead of a blocked task right after you granted it runs and called start. It answers at once only when nothing\n        in the org can run, and ends early when the last thing that could run stops. Use it when the user wants to be\n        told when the work is done: tell them you will wait now, call progress(wait_seconds=...) and repeat as long\n        as they want (a turn cannot notify them later). Never poll with shell sleeps. Without wait_seconds it answers\n        \"status?\".\n        ",
      "input_schema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "task_id": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "type": "string"
              },
              {
                "type": "null"
              }
            ],
            "default": null,
            "title": "Task Id"
          },
          "wait_seconds": {
            "default": 0,
            "title": "Wait Seconds",
            "type": "integer"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "not_found": [],
